We construct a pilot vector as
$\mathbf p_{qk}=[P_{qk0},\ldots,P_{qk,N-1}]^{\mathsf T}
\in\mathbb C^N$, $k=1,\ldots,K$, $q=0,\ldots,Q-1$.

The time-domain transmitted signal is generated through the inverse
discrete affine Fourier transform (IDAFT) applied to $\mathbf p_{qk}$,
mathematically formulated as \cite{ding2026}
\begin{equation}
 s_{qk}[t]=\frac{1}{\sqrt N}\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}
 P_{qkn}e^{j2\pi(c_1t^2+c_2n^2+nt/N)},
 \label{eq:afdm-time-signal}
\end{equation}
where $P_{qkn}$ represents the $n$th symbol of $\mathbf p_{qk}$.

The signal in \eqref{eq:afdm-time-signal} can be represented in matrix
form as
\[
 \mathbf s_{qk}=\mathbf F_{\rm A}^{\mathsf H}\mathbf p_{qk},
\]
where
\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \mathbf F_{\rm A}
 =\left[\frac{1}{\sqrt N}e^{-j2\pi(c_2r^2+rt/N+c_1t^2)}
 \right]_{r,t=0}^{N-1}
 \end{gathered}
 \label{eq:daft}
\end{equation}
represents the discrete affine Fourier transform (DAFT) matrix.
